.TH "ELSERENO\-SCOPE" "5" "2026\-04\-19" "ElSereno scope file" "File formats"
.SH NAME
\f[B]scope.yaml\f[R]: authorised scope file for \f[I]elsereno\f[R](1)
.SH DESCRIPTION
A \f[I]scope.yaml\f[R] file defines the authorised ranges and ports, and
the dialling blacklist, for an engagement.
It also accepts protocols, web binds and a canary, which are parsed but
not applied.
.PP
A scope file applies only when passed with \f[B]\-\-scope\f[R] (to
\f[B]scan\f[R], to \f[B]serve\f[R] for dashboard scans, and to
\f[B]dial\f[R] in the offensive build; \f[B]lint \-\-scope\f[R] only
validates it).
No other command takes it, so it does not limit \f[B]discover\f[R],
\f[B]fingerprint probe\f[R], \f[B]plaintext\-check\f[R], \f[B]tui
\-\-input\f[R], the per\-protocol verbs, or the offensive
\f[B]write\f[R], \f[B]exploit\f[R], \f[B]proxy listen\f[R],
\f[B]harvest\f[R] and \f[B]creds\-check\f[R].
Nothing loads it on its own: a \f[I]scope.yaml\f[R] in the working
directory is not read, and without \f[B]\-\-scope\f[R] no target is
filtered.
Keys other than the ones below are a parse error.
