.SH SEMANTICS
.TP
\f[B]ranges\f[R]
CIDRs, IPv4 and IPv6.
With no entries, any address passes.
With entries, a target outside all of them is dropped before any
connection.
A malformed CIDR is skipped without error, so what only it covered is
dropped.
.TP
\f[B]ports.allow / ports.deny\f[R]
Deny wins over allow.
If \f[B]allow\f[R] is empty, all ports are allowed subject to
\f[B]deny\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]dial.blocked_numbers\f[R]
Numbers refused by \f[B]dial\f[R], exact or as a prefix, on top of the
hard\-coded block of numbers of 3 digits or fewer.
\f[B]dial\f[R] normalises the dialled number (drops \(dq+\(dq, a leading
\(dq00\(dq and punctuation) but not the entries, so write them as digits
only: \(dq+34112\(dq or \(dq0034112\(dq block nothing.
.TP
\f[B]protocols.allow / protocols.deny\f[R]
Parsed, not applied: they do not limit which plugins run.
.TP
\f[B]binds.allow\f[R]
Parsed, not applied.
A non\-loopback bind of \f[B]serve\f[R] is governed by its own flags
(\f[B]\-\-tls\-cert\f[R], \f[B]\-\-tls\-key\f[R],
\f[B]\-\-i\-know\-what\-im\-doing\f[R]).
.TP
\f[B]canary\f[R]
Parsed, not applied.
.PP
A dropped target is not probed and leaves no trace: there is no
per\-target message and no audit event.
When no target is left, \f[B]scan\f[R] exits with \(dqno targets after
scope filter\(dq.
